From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bernardo Pereira <bpereira@cloudflare.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 11:00:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Bound repeated SVG background work with a node-visit budget

A repeated SVG background re-renders its whole vector tree for every
tile, and the per-layer 4096-tile cap multiplies with the tree's node
count, the layer count and the element count. The reported input (40
elements, 1px tiles, 200 paths) is about 33M node visits, roughly half
an hour of synchronous paint.

`paint_scene_bounded` now meters that work: one unit per usvg node per
tile, charged before a layer is drawn. A layer the remaining budget
cannot afford is skipped whole, and the rest of the page still paints,
so a screenshot always produces a frame. `paint_scene` stays unmetered,
so recording, PDF and canvas output still paint everything.

The budget is calibrated against the per-layer cap rather than the
viewport: 4096 tiles * 128 nodes = 524,288 units, so any layer the cap
admits with up to 128 usvg nodes always fits, and several ordinary
patterns fit alongside it (two full-viewport 24px tiles of a 100-node
tree at 465k, or ~68 496px 8px dot layers at 7.7k each). Measured
end-to-end on the core test harness (debug host build, 1440x900 output):
a page at the budget takes 5.1 s with 34-node tiles and 9.8 s with
132-node tiles; one full-viewport 24px 100-node pattern (232,560 units)
takes 6.9 s, and an 8px dot pattern takes 1.0 s for one 496px layer and
1.4 s for eight. The 6.6M-unit pathological page takes 0.49 s because
every layer is skipped, so the budget bounds it to 1/64 of its unbounded
work. A smaller budget would skip full-viewport patterns of realistic
node counts; a larger one only lengthens the worst case, since a single
capped layer already costs seconds at these node counts.

Measuring a layer is work too, so it is charged with the layer: the walk
stops once it passes the nodes the remaining budget could still afford,
and a layer that does not fit pays for the visits it made. Otherwise N
elements sharing one unaffordable tree would walk it N times for free;
this way measuring and drawing together stay inside the budget (800
elements sharing one 6001-node tree over 10x10 tiles leave 120 units,
which is why the dot pattern after them is skipped). The walk is
iterative and keeps one frame per open group, so tree depth costs heap
rather than stack. A subdocument is painted by a second painter, so it
inherits the remaining budget and hands the rest back: an iframe would
otherwise repaint the same unaffordable layer in full.

A text node is one tree node but one outline per glyph, and usvg keeps
it as a single `Text` node with a flattened glyph group in which
consecutive glyphs share one path, so the count charges that group and
one unit per character. 200 glyphs over 3969 tiles is 810k units and is
skipped; charging the two tree nodes alone would paint it.

Raster tile fills are not charged: their cost is dominated by covered
area, which the viewport already bounds, so tile count is a poor proxy
(20k 8px fills took 4.1 s against 1.5 s for 324k 2px fills).
